# Contrarian Brief — Against a Quiet-by-Design Standard for MWAA Terminals

**Run:** Quiet-by-Design Standards for MWAA Terminals
**Author:** Contrarian
**Date:** 2026-08-07
**Purpose:** Argue the strongest reasonable case against the run's central thesis. Not a strawman; the position the Council must survive if the standard is worth publishing.

---

## Executive line

MWAA is being asked to adopt a common quiet-by-design standard for new and substantially renovated terminal space at IAD and DCA, at the exact moment its most important operational fact is a mass-fatality accident at DCA in which controllers were, per the NTSB, "overwhelmed" by traffic and situational-awareness collapsed. In that political and safety environment, the operator that reduces the salience of audible information in its terminals — even under the sophisticated framing of "intelligible localized audio and redundant visual channels" — is the operator that gets asked, in the next Congressional hearing, whether it prioritized ambient quietness over passenger and employee ability to hear a warning. The thesis is not wrong because "quiet is bad." It is wrong because it (a) misidentifies where the intelligibility problem actually lives (in NFPA 72 EVACS design, which is already binding), (b) understates the cost and governance burden of the specification-and-commissioning regime it requires, (c) misreads MWAA's contractual authority over carriers under the 2025 Use and Lease Agreement, and (d) inherits a European operating model that has produced documented passenger harm, consumer-court complaints, and, at several named peers, quiet reversals. If MWAA wants better acoustics, it should fix EVACS intelligibility to code, tighten reverberation-time targets in a design-manual amendment, and stop there. The rest of the "quiet by design" apparatus is scope creep.

---

## 1. The strongest version of the counterargument (six bullets)

1. **The thesis is temporally wrong.** DCA is inside a heightened-safety political envelope following the 29 January 2025 midair collision that killed 67 people. The NTSB's 50 recommendations and the Board's finding that controllers were "overwhelmed" and suffered "reduced situational awareness" make this the wrong decade to codify any operator policy that will be read as reducing audible information at DCA. Timing, in a political operating environment, is a governance variable, not a soft one.

2. **The intelligibility problem is already solved by binding code.** NFPA 72 requires that emergency voice/alarm communications systems (EVACS) meet an average Speech Transmission Index of 0.50, with 90 percent of measurement locations in each acoustically distinguishable space at STI ≥ 0.45. Airport terminals in the U.S. are governed by NFPA 72 through local building-code adoption. If MWAA wants intelligibility, it should enforce the code it already owes. A parallel "quiet by design" regime layered on top of NFPA 72 creates a compliance stack with no additional safety value and new litigation surface.

3. **MWAA cannot enforce PA discipline on carriers.** The 15-year Use and Lease Agreement effective 1 January 2025 runs through 2039. Airline-controlled boarding announcements at leased gates are the loudest recurring PA source in any concourse. The moment to build carrier-facing announcement governance into a lease was the 2024 negotiation. That door closed on 31 December 2024. A quiet standard adopted in 2026 will bind MWAA staff and A/E consultants; it will not bind United at IAD or American at DCA except through amendment or new gate-lease supplements the airlines have no reason to sign.

4. **"Silent airport" is a branding concept with a mixed operational record.** London City's silent-airport policy coexists with documented passenger complaints about missed flights and inadequate operational communication. The European Consumer Centre Austria publishes formal guidance warning travelers that at silent airports "the passenger is not informed that the gate had changed and missed his or her flight" is a common claim category, and that "the airport is not obliged to make announcements … in order to guarantee that every single passenger receives the information relevant to him." That is a documented externality shifted from the operator to the passenger, not a passenger-experience win.

5. **The digital-redundancy story fails the actual passenger distribution.** The run prompt correctly names accessibility, digital exclusion, and infrequent travelers as risks. It then proposes to solve them with visual FIDS, mobile notifications, muted TVs, and staffed communication — the same "redundant channels" that airports currently rely on. Those channels are not neutral: they systematically disadvantage older adults, non-English speakers, people with low vision, people with cognitive processing differences, and any passenger whose phone battery has died. IAD's international passenger share was 10.5 million in CY 2025 — roughly one in three passengers is international, and a material fraction do not have U.S. carrier apps installed or T-Mobile roaming turned on.

6. **The opportunity cost is nine figures of program attention.** MWAA has committed to a $20–22.5B Dulles program over a decade and holds ~$4.9B in outstanding revenue debt. Concourse E opens in autumn 2026. The mobile lounges are being retired. Every hour of MWAA Engineering, VP-Airports, and Design Review attention spent litigating STI thresholds, PA zoning, TV-mute policies, and tenant-technology standards is an hour not spent on the gate-throughput, curb-to-gate journey, and airside redesign decisions that actually move the operator's traffic and rate-base math. A quiet-by-design standard is a decorative program in the middle of a structural one.

