## 2. Evidence

### 2.1 The regulatory floor already exists — NFPA 72

The intelligibility problem the thesis actually cares about is a code-driven, technical problem. NFPA 72 (2010 edition and forward) codified quantitative STI requirements for emergency voice/alarm communications systems: acceptable if at least 90 percent of measurement locations within each acoustically distinguishable space have STI ≥ 0.45 and the ADS average is STI ≥ 0.50 [Source: https://www.commercialintegrator.com/security/mass_notification/mass-notification-intelligibility/]. This is not a "should"; through local building-code adoption it is enforced during commissioning. An MWAA design manual that restates NFPA 72 is redundant; one that goes beyond it invites a compliance stack. The measurement methodology (STI vs. STIPA) is defined in IEC 60268-16 and produces reproducible field values, but STIPA measurement across a large-format terminal is non-trivial: a documented terminal case reports **"USD $2.8 million in remediation, eight weeks of disruption, and immeasurable passenger frustration"** when acoustic STI predictions were overridden by architectural intent [Source: https://acousplan.com/blog/iec-60268-16-sti-measurement]. That is the cost of getting the acoustic-architectural coordination wrong once. The remedy is not a new standard; it is discipline against acoustic-consultant overrides — which requires no new document.

### 2.2 The 29 January 2025 DCA collision changed the political frame

The NTSB completed its investigation and issued 50 safety recommendations, finding that the accident resulted from "the placement of a helicopter route in close proximity to a runway approach path without procedural mitigations, combined with failures by multiple organizations to identify and address known risks," and that on the night in question controllers were "overwhelmed" and suffered "reduced situational awareness" [Source: https://enotrans.org/article/ntsb-unveils-findings-probable-cause-and-recommendations-from-dca-mid-air-collision-investigation/]. The Board's urgent recommendations targeted runways 15/33 and helicopter Route 4. Congressional attention to DCA operational safety is now sustained. Any MWAA-branded document that reads as reducing audible information in a DCA terminal — even one that carefully distinguishes "quiet" from "silent" — will be quoted back at MWAA in the next incident.

### 2.3 Silent-airport peers have documented failure modes

The European Consumer Centre Austria — a state-funded consumer-protection body, not a trade advocate — publishes formal advisory guidance for travelers navigating silent airports: **"the passenger was not informed that the gate had changed and missed his or her flight, so the most common claim. Most airports actually only display such changes on monitors and/or send notifications to the passenger's smartphones,"** and **"the airport is not obliged to make announcements via speakers in order to guarantee that every single passenger receives the information relevant to him"** [Source: https://europakonsument.at/en/page/tips-silent-airports]. London City Airport's own silent policy page confirms announcements are made only in emergencies [Source: https://www.londoncityairport.com/at-the-airport/need-to-know/airport-policies/silent-policy]; independent review aggregators document sustained operational complaints about missed flights, inadequate delay updates, and screens failing to reflect real-time delays [Source: https://ca.trustpilot.com/review/londoncityairport.com]. This is not a "European best practice" the U.S. should transfer; it is a design choice with a known and adverse externality distribution.

### 2.4 ADA and DOT already require visual counterparts — the marginal accessibility gain is small

The DOT ADA rule for airports and the ABA Accessibility Standards require assistive-listening systems in assembly areas and visual counterparts to audible information. Passengers who identify as needing visual or hearing assistance must receive **"prompt access to the same trip information as other passengers at the gate, ticket area, customer service desk, and on the aircraft"** [Source: https://www.transportation.gov/airconsumer/disabilitybillofrights]. A visual-first environment does not, by itself, satisfy the DOT requirement; the accessibility argument in favor of quiet-by-design is a restatement of law that already binds. Meanwhile the population being harmed by an audible-first policy — deaf, hard-of-hearing, cognitively different, low-vision — is not the population being helped by a quieter concourse. The quiet-preferring population overlaps heavily with autism-spectrum, sensory-processing, and dementia-affected travelers, whose documented intervention of choice is not concourse-wide quiet but the **dedicated sensory room** already deployed at Phoenix, Seattle, Pittsburgh, Atlanta, and Shannon [Source: https://www.portseattle.org/blog/quiet-oasis-stimulating-environment; https://acl.gov/TransportationCenter/promising-practices/airport-sensory-rooms]. That is a room, not a standard.

### 2.5 Airline PA control is contractual, and the contract is fresh

The 2025 U&L took effect 1 January 2025 and runs through calendar 2039, adopted alongside a $9.0B joint capital plan (~$7.0B IAD, ~$2.39B DCA) [context packet §3]. Airline-controlled gate announcements at leased premises are the loudest recurring PA source in any concourse. Vendor-side reporting on automated boarding-announcement systems (AviaVox, Simpleway) makes plain that carriers, not operators, drive gate-side call cadence, script, and volume, and that **"some airports don't allow automated gate announcements because their PA systems are outdated and incompatible, and fire marshals may disallow proprietary public address systems to preserve their ability to override them for emergency announcements"** [Source: https://viewfromthewing.com/american-airlines-has-updated-its-boarding-announcements/; https://simpleway.global/airports-us]. Any MWAA standard that requires carriers to change gate-agent behavior is a change to the 2025 U&L. The MII and signatory-list terms of that agreement have not been verified in the context packet [context packet §12], but a hybrid U&L is not amendable at operator convenience.

### 2.6 The "quiet" claim at Schiphol is confounded

Schiphol's noise reduction is driven overwhelmingly by aircraft-fleet quieting and airport-charge differentiation, not by terminal PA policy: aircraft averaged 2.09 dB quieter in 2025 vs. 2018 [Source: https://news.schiphol.com/schiphol-in-2025-quieter-aircraft-more-satisfied-travellers-and-solid-financial-results/]. Community noise complaints against Schiphol still increased 7 percent year over year in the 2023 reporting period [Source: https://www.dutchnews.nl/2024/04/noise-complaints-against-schiphol-increased-by-7-last-year/]. The often-cited passenger-experience improvement is not attributable to concourse announcement policy. Citing Schiphol as evidence for quiet-by-design terminal standards is a category error: it is evidence for fleet-quieting via airport-charge differentiation, an instrument MWAA does not use and which the U.S. rates-and-charges framework and airline U&Ls do not support.

### 2.7 Standardizing IAD and DCA is a category error

The two airports are not the same operational animal. IAD is a widebody, international, connecting hub with a Saarinen main terminal (1962, National Historic Landmark), Pelli midfield C/D targeted for replacement, and a 435,000 sq ft greenfield Concourse E opening autumn 2026 [context packet §§4, 9]. DCA is a slot-controlled, perimeter-restricted, origin-and-destination airport whose Terminal 1 dates to 1941 (Goodman, National Register) and Terminal 2 to 1997 (Pelli National Hall, vaulted volumes that make reverberation control inherently harder) [context packet §9]. Historic-review overlays constrain any acoustic intervention inside the IAD main terminal envelope and DCA Terminal 1. A "common MWAA standard" that treats both airports the same either (a) sets the bar low enough to fit both, in which case it is unenforceable at Concourse E's greenfield opportunity, or (b) sets it high enough to matter at IAD, in which case it is unbuildable at DCA without disfiguring Pelli's National Hall.

