## 3. Specific scenarios where the thesis is wrong

### Scenario A — DCA Terminal 1 refresh, 2028

MWAA adopts the quiet-by-design standard. A subsequent T1 refresh RFP requires ambient dB, RT60, and STI targets consistent with the standard. Bidders return costed alternatives showing that the 1941 Goodman envelope cannot achieve target reverberation without dropped acoustic ceilings that Section 106 review will not approve. The project is delayed 14 months while MWAA either seeks a Programmatic Agreement variance or grants a project-specific exception. The standard's "context-dependent options" tier absorbs the exception, and the standard becomes advisory. The cost of the delay is borne by rates and charges.

### Scenario B — Post-incident DCA hearing, 2029

An irregular-operations event at DCA — a weather diversion cascade, a security incident, a de-icing overrun — leaves several thousand passengers stranded overnight. Post-event reporting includes passenger accounts that gate changes were displayed on FIDS but not audibly announced, that passengers with dead phones missed rebooking windows, and that non-English-speaking international transfer passengers could not locate their assigned rebooking desks. The 2026 MWAA quiet-by-design standard is quoted in local coverage as "the airport authority's policy of minimizing audible announcements." MWAA's response cannot cite Schiphol.

### Scenario C — Concourse E opens, 2026

The Concourse E gate program is already procured and built to design intent that predates any quiet standard adopted in 2026. Retrofit to a subsequent MWAA standard requires PA rework at a facility not yet six months old. United, the anchor tenant with a separate long-term lease and its own gate-agent scripts and Auracast-style gate-audio pilot, declines to modify its announcement practice. The standard covers MWAA-owned finishes and PA infrastructure; the actual audible experience at E does not change.

### Scenario D — The standard covers the wrong problem

Post-opening acoustic measurement at IAD Concourses A and B reveals that the dominant acoustic problem is not concourse PA volume but reverberation from hard-surfaced tile floors and glass curtain walls, plus baggage-belt noise at the FIS. The quiet standard has driven MWAA to invest in PA-zoning hardware and directional loudspeakers. The 3-decibel improvement passengers perceive is delivered instead by a $1.4M FIS baggage-belt liner and a soft-surface flooring change at two concessions food courts — interventions that would have happened without any standard, if MWAA Engineering had run a $60,000 post-occupancy acoustic survey first.

