## 5. The concession the thesis should make

The strongest defensible retreat is to narrow the standard to two things MWAA actually owns and can enforce inside its own capital program:

- **A design-manual amendment on room acoustics and EVACS intelligibility.** Explicit RT60 (reverberation time) targets by space type, an explicit STIPA measurement protocol at commissioning, and an explicit prohibition on architectural overrides of acoustic-consultant STI predictions without written CEO delegation. This is enforceable through the A/E contract, does not touch tenants or airlines, and has code alignment (NFPA 72) that gives it legal ground.

- **A sensory-room and quiet-space requirement for terminal programs above a defined size threshold.** This is the intervention with the best documented outcome for the affected population, is cheap, does not require concourse-wide PA policy, and is already a peer practice at Phoenix, Seattle, Pittsburgh, Atlanta, and Shannon.

Everything else — announcement-frequency governance, TV-mute policy, PA zoning philosophy, "quiet" as a passenger-experience metric, tenant-technology standardization — should be dropped. It cannot be enforced against airlines under the 2025 U&L, it exposes MWAA to post-incident political risk at DCA, it duplicates code, and it delivers a passenger-experience improvement that is not attributable to the intervention.

If the Council is going to publish, publish that narrower thing. A "quiet by design" standard that survives its first NTSB hearing at DCA is not a quiet standard. It is an intelligibility-and-sensory-space standard. Call it what it is.

